THE PROBLEM IT SOLVES

Most inbound lead-handling systems are held together by manual triage: someone checks a form submissions inbox, manually looks up the company, manually decides how urgent it is, manually creates a CRM record, and manually drafts a reply, often hours after the lead actually submitted the form. By the time that happens, speed-to-lead (one of the strongest predictors of conversion) is already lost.

AROE removes every one of those manual steps without removing judgment from the process, an AI agent reads the actual inquiry and reasons about urgency and fit the way a human SDR would, rather than relying on rigid form dropdowns.

ARCHITECTURE OVERVIEW

```
Tally Form Submission
        │
        ▼
 Revenue Orchestrator (parent workflow)
        │
        ├─▶ Contact Manager        (child workflow)
        ├─▶ Company Manager        (child workflow)
        ├─▶ AI Qualification Agent (child workflow)
        ├─▶ Company Enrichment Agent (child workflow)
        │
        ├─▶ Lead Scoring        (inline)
        ├─▶ Deal Manager        (inline)
        │
        ├─▶ Communication Agent (child workflow)
        ├─▶ Meeting Scheduler   (child workflow)
        └─▶ Analytics & Audit   (child workflow, called from every stage)

 Error Trigger Workflow (standalone, referenced by every workflow's settings)
        └─▶ Analytics & Audit + Slack alert on any failure

```

Design principle: workflows that are genuinely reusable or independently useful (identity resolution, communication, scheduling, logging) are separate child workflows, callable from anywhere. Workflows that are purely linear, single-purpose steps with no reuse case (scoring, deal creation) are consolidated inline into the orchestrator. This was a deliberate architectural decision made partway through the build, see Key Engineering Decisions.

WORKFLOW BREAKDOWN

1. Revenue Orchestrator (parent)
Receives the Tally submission, validates the payload, and sequences every downstream call. Owns the full lead journey end to end and is the only workflow that knows the complete order of operations.
2. Contact Manager
Given a name, email, and company, checks HubSpot for an existing contact by email, updates it if found or creates it if not, then ensures the associated company record exists and is linked. Built as a reusable identity-resolution layer, any future intake channel (a different form, a WhatsApp lead, a CSV import) can call this same workflow without modification.
3. Company Manager
Same identity-resolution pattern as Contact Manager, applied to the Company object, matched by domain.
4. AI Qualification Agent
An LLM-backed agent (Groq, Llama 3.3 70B) reads the lead's free-text inquiry and returns a structured classification: requested services, urgency, a one-sentence intent summary, and a budget indicator. Every value is validated against a fixed allow-list before being written to HubSpot, since AI output, even when prompted for strict JSON, occasionally drifts in format.
5. Company Enrichment Agent
Given only a company name, this agent uses Tavily (a search API purpose-built for AI agents) to research the company live, then has an LLM synthesize the raw research into a structured profile: industry, estimated size, and a plain-English summary. Chosen specifically because traditional firmographic APIs (e.g. Clearbit) have discontinued their free tiers, this agent produces comparable enrichment at zero cost by researching directly rather than querying a paid data vendor.
6. Lead Scoring (inline)
A deterministic scoring function, not another AI call, combining urgency, budget signal, company size, and industry fit into a Hot / Warm / Cold verdict. Kept rule-based rather than AI-based deliberately, for consistent, explainable scoring.
7. Deal Manager (inline)
Creates a HubSpot deal at a pipeline stage matched to the lead's score, associates it with both the contact and company, and, for Hot leads specifically, creates and assigns a same-day follow-up task.
8. Communication Agent
Drafts a personalized follow-up email referencing the lead's actual stated need (not a generic template), sends it via Gmail, and posts a Slack alert to the team when a Hot lead has just been contacted.
9. Meeting Scheduler
For Hot leads only: checks real Google Calendar availability across the next five business days, books the first open 30-minute slot automatically, and advances the deal to the next pipeline stage.
10. Analytics & Audit
A shared logging workflow, called from every stage of the pipeline (and from the error handler below). Appends execution ID, workflow name, status, and a full JSON snapshot to a Google Sheet, giving a complete audit trail of every lead's journey through the system.
11. Error Handling (standalone)
A single Error Trigger-based workflow, referenced in every other workflow's settings. Any unhandled failure anywhere in the pipeline routes here automatically, gets logged to Analytics & Audit with a failed status, and triggers an immediate Slack alert, so a broken step surfaces in seconds, not when someone eventually notices a lead never got a reply.

