KEY ENGINEERING DECISIONS

HTTP and native API nodes don't carry your payload forward. Every HTTP Request node (and several native nodes) in n8n return only their own API response, not whatever data flowed into them. Any workflow step that needs to read earlier context after an
API call has to explicitly re-attach it, typically via a small Code node referencing an earlier node by name ($('NodeName').item.json). This turned out to be the single most common source of bugs during the build, and the fix pattern is now applied consistently 
everywhere it's needed.

Native node schema loading isn't always reliable. HubSpot's native n8n node failed to load custom contact properties into its property picker, even after confirming the properties existed and all relevant API scopes (including schema-read scopes) were granted. 
The reliable workaround: direct HTTP Request nodes calling HubSpot's REST API with Bearer token authentication, bypassing the property picker entirely. The native node is still used for core record creation (Contact, Company, Deal), since that functionality worked correctly.

Consolidate where reuse doesn't exist. The original architecture treated every logical step as its own child workflow. Partway through the build, Lead Scoring and Deal Manager were folded directly into the Orchestrator as inline nodes, since neither had any 
realistic reuse case outside this single pipeline, every added Execute Workflow boundary was pure overhead (another node-name reference to keep straight, another place for data to silently drop) with no architectural benefit. Communication Agent and Meeting
Scheduler remained separate child workflows since the main canvas was becoming difficult to navigate at that scale. The result is a system that's modular where modularity earns its cost, and linear where it doesn't.

Free-tier constraints as a starting condition, not an afterthought. HubSpot's free CRM caps custom properties at 10 per object, every property schema in this build was designed against that ceiling from the start. Company enrichment was re-architected
around Tavily specifically because the originally planned data vendor no longer offers free access.
